# Vocabulary — "template repo" vs ".tmpl file"

"The template repo" and "the .tmpl file" are different things — do not conflate them.

| Phrase | Refers to |
|---|---|
| "the template repo", "at the template level", "in the template", "to the template" | this repo — the **public upstream `claude-settings-template`** at `~/Repos/claude-settings-template/`, independent git remote, the seed that other people / future machines clone. |
| "the .tmpl file", "the template file", "in the .tmpl" | a hydration source file (`*.tmpl`) inside *any* repo — e.g. `settings.json.tmpl`, `CLAUDE.md.tmpl`. Source consumed by `hydrate.sh`. |

**Default interpretation: "template" = this repo, NOT a file.**

When the user asks "should this go in the template?" they are asking whether the change
belongs in this public seed repo so it ships to anyone provisioning from it — they are NOT
asking whether to edit a `.tmpl` file in some other repo.

A change can need to land in **both**: the user's personal `claude-settings`/`.claude/`
clone (so it hydrates locally) AND this template repo (so the public seed ships with it).
These are two distinct edits in two distinct git repos with two distinct push targets.

If genuinely ambiguous, ask.

# Config repos

- **Generated files.** A file with a `.tmpl` sibling is a hydrated output: edit the `.tmpl`, preview with
  `hydrate.sh --diff`, apply (`~/.claude`: `scripts/apply-settings.sh`; `~/dotfiles`: `hydrate.sh`), commit the
  `.tmpl`; paths in `settings.json.tmpl` use `~`, never absolute or `$HOME`. A direct edit to an output (`CLAUDE.md`,
  `~/.zshrc`, `~/.gitconfig`) is lost on the next hydrate. `settings.json` is exempt: a direct edit stays on this
  machine only (`hooks` excepted) and never reaches git. Hydrate merges settings: delete a key or entry
  via `__remove__` in the `.tmpl`.
- **Commits.** After editing a tracked file in `~/.claude`, commit it by explicit path (not memory files) and push.
- **Local overlays.** Personal additions go in `~/.gitconfig.local` or `~/.ssh/config.local`, never in a `.tmpl`.
- **Memory writes.** Never put secret-shaped values (account IDs, registry hosts, ARNs, key material, tokens) in
  memory files: write a placeholder such as `<aws-account-id>` or omit the detail.
- **dotfiles.** [dotfiles](__DOTFILES_REPO_URL__) (`~/dotfiles`) is managed with GNU Stow: stowed files are symlinks
  into `~/dotfiles`, so after editing one, commit and push there. After installing or removing a Homebrew package,
  regenerate the Brewfile as `~/dotfiles/CLAUDE.md` describes.

# Preferences

## Code Style
- Indentation: 4 spaces
- Max line length: 120 characters
- Comments: a brief summary doc comment on public/exported APIs; elsewhere only a non-obvious why. This overrides
  the harness's no-comments default for public APIs only.
- When creating a new repository, add `.gitignore` (build artefacts, IDE files), `.gitattributes` (line endings) and
  `.editorconfig`.

## Argument/Parameter Formatting
- All arguments/parameters on same line, OR each on its own line (including first)
- If list has more than 3 items: each on its own line
- If list exceeds 120 characters: each on its own line
- Example (inline):
  ```
  DoSomething(arg1, arg2, arg3);
  ```
- Example (wrapped):
  ```
  DoSomething(
      arg1,
      arg2,
      arg3,
      arg4);
  ```

## Communication
- Be terse, formal, direct
- I am an experienced expert software engineer

## Behaviour
- Don't guess. If unsure, search using MCPs, the `web-search` skill, or ask the user
- When you need URLs, documentation or current information, use the `web-search` skill rather than guessing or
  declining; it queries `$SEARXNG_URL` (see the README).
- When a superpowers phase completes (end of brainstorming, planning or an implementation phase) and it is a natural
  point to reset context, offer `/handover` — only at these phase seams.

## Repo Housekeeping (always while we're here)

When working on any of my repos, treat dependency, action, runner, and IaC-vulnerability
freshness as part of the job — surface it during brainstorming/design, not as an afterthought.
In design discussions, ask up front whether the housekeeping ships in the same PR as the
feature or as a separate small PR landing first (default: separate first).

- **NuGet (or other package manager) dependencies** — bump everything to the latest GA. Use
  `dotnet list package --outdated` (or the equivalent) rather than guessing. Check
  `Directory.Packages.props` (CPM) first if it exists, otherwise scan all `*.csproj`.
- **GitHub Actions** — audit `.github/workflows/*.yml` for pinned old majors and bump
  `uses: org/action@vN` to the latest stable major.
- **Workflow runners** — move `runs-on:` to the latest supported (`ubuntu-24.04`,
  `windows-2025`, etc.).
- **Trivy / IaC** — fix outstanding findings on Dockerfiles, Terraform, Kubernetes/Helm, and
  CFN. The `code-review-suite:trivy-reviewer` agent or `trivy config` is the source of truth.

Keep the housekeeping focused — don't silently include unrelated refactors.

## Temporary Files
- Put every temporary file in this session's directory, `/tmp/claude-<session_id>/`. The SessionStart hook creates
  it, exports it to Bash as `$CLAUDE_TEMP_DIR`, and puts its literal path in context for Write/Edit paths; the guard
  hooks deny other temp locations.
- When spawning a subagent, pass the literal path in its prompt (e.g. `use /tmp/claude-<id>/ for temp files`).
- Clean up your temp files when no longer needed.

## Background work
- Run long Bash commands and agent dispatches that don't gate the next step with `run_in_background: true`; you are
  notified on completion.

## Agents
- Always set the `name` parameter when dispatching agents via the Agent tool
- Names must be kebab-case, descriptive, and unique within a session
- For predefined agents with definition files: use the agent definition name
  (e.g., `security-reviewer`, `code-analysis`)
- For built-in agent types: use `{type}-{scope}` (e.g., `explore-auth-flow`,
  `plan-api-redesign`)
- For task-scoped agents: use `{role}-task-{n}` (e.g., `implementer-task-3`,
  `spec-reviewer-task-3`)
- This enables `SendMessage({to: name})` for diagnosing agent failures or
  assessing results while agents run
- When dispatching a subagent, state in its spawn prompt that its plain-text
  output is invisible to the controller and it must deliver its entire result via
  `SendMessage` to main — subagents otherwise go idle without ever reporting back

## Bash
- Never use compound shell commands (`&&`, `||`, `;`) — execute each command as a separate Bash call
- Never use command substitution (`$(...)`, backticks) — capture output from one Bash call and pass it to the next
- Never use subshells or grouping (`(...)`, `{ ...; }`) — use separate Bash calls
- Prefer dedicated tools or separate Bash calls over piping (`|`) where possible
- Prefer dedicated tools or separate Bash calls over redirection (`>`, `>>`) where possible; `2>&1` is acceptable when capturing stderr
- **Carve-out:** the `git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF' … EOF\n)"` HEREDOC is permitted — it
  carries a literal multi-line commit message that the rules above would otherwise force
  into a clumsy temp-file handoff. This is the ONLY exemption `bash-guard.sh` recognises.
  For PR bodies always use `gh ... --body-file <path>` (write the body to
  `${CLAUDE_TEMP_DIR}/pr-body.md` first).
- These Bash rules are load-bearing, not stylistic: `hooks/secret-bash-guard.sh` screens each
  pipeline stage by its first word, which is only sound because `bash-guard.sh` denies every
  other compound form except unquoted `(...)` grouping. Never relax one without the other.

## LSP
- Prefer LSP (`goToDefinition`, `findReferences`, `goToImplementation`, `incomingCalls`, `outgoingCalls`) over Grep when positioned at a specific symbol and need semantic precision
- Fall back to Grep/Glob for discovery, broad text searches, config files, and non-code files
- If LSP calls fail or no language server serves the language, say so and suggest installing one

## Playwright
- Prefer the `playwright-cli` skill over `mcp__playwright__*` tools — it is less token expensive
- If the Playwright Chrome extension is installed, the CLI can attach to an existing Chrome

## Git
- Do not add Co-Authored-By trailers to commits
- Do not add Claude Code advertising to PR descriptions
- PR descriptions must begin with a brief contextual summary (1–3 sentences) that orients
  the reader: what broader initiative or goal this PR contributes to, where it fits in any
  sequence of related PRs, and why the change is needed now. Write this for a non-technical
  audience. Follow this with the detailed technical list of changes, including links to
  related PRs where informative or clarifying.
